TASK 2 — mouth instructions clarification: harness/chief.py
============================================================
`MOUTH_INSTRUCTIONS` (the system prompt for the realtime voice model) currently tells
the mouth "for every single thing he says" call `ask_chief`. Task 3 below will make the
browser inject Chief's LATER sentences as a synthetic `conversation.item.create` message
with `role: "user"` and text starting `"(Chief continues:) "` — that is NOT something
Neill said, it's more of Chief's own answer arriving in a second piece, and the mouth
must not mistake it for a new request and call `ask_chief` on it again (which would
loop/misbehave).

Add a short new paragraph to `MOUTH_INSTRUCTIONS`, right after the existing "NEVER
answer anything yourself..." paragraph, in the same plain, short style as the rest of
that string:

    "Sometimes you'll see a message that starts with "(Chief continues:)" — that isn't
    Neill talking. It's the rest of Chief's answer arriving in a second piece. Just say
    it naturally, like you're still mid-sentence from before. Don't call ask_chief on
    it, and don't treat it like a new question."

Keep it to one short paragraph. Do not touch `CHIEF_PROMPT` or `ASK_CHIEF_TOOL` or
anything else in this file.

============================================================
TASK 3 — harness/web/voice.html
============================================================
In the `handle()` function's `response.function_call_arguments.done` / `ask_chief`
branch (currently does one `fetch('/api/voice/ask', ...)` and waits for the full JSON):

Replace the single fetch-and-wait with a call to `fetch('/api/voice/ask/stream', {
method: 'POST', headers: {'Content-Type': 'application/json'}, body: JSON.stringify({
said: args.said || lastSaid, context: lastAnswer }) })` and consume the response body as
an SSE stream using `resp.body.getReader()` + `TextDecoder`, splitting on blank lines
(`\n\n`) into individual `event: <name>\ndata: <json>` records, same way the backend
formats them. Do this with plain code (no new library — none is available in this
static HTML file; keep it dependency-free like the rest of the file).

Required behaviour:

1. FIRST `sentence` event received: remove the "thinking…" holding bubble (`hold`, as
   today), send it back to the mouth exactly like today's code does — a
   `conversation.item.create` of `{type: 'function_call_output', call_id: ev.call_id,
   output: JSON.stringify({ answer: <that sentence's text> })}` followed by
   `dc.send(JSON.stringify({ type: 'response.create' }))`. This is what makes the mouth
   start speaking almost immediately instead of waiting for the whole answer. Track that
   a `response.create` was just sent (see point 4, the busy flag) so later sentences
   know to wait.
   Also call `turn('chief', 'Chief', <that sentence's text>)` so the transcript panel
   shows it right away (do this for every sentence event, first or later).

2. EACH LATER `sentence` event (second onward, before `done` arrives): push the
   sentence's text onto a queue instead of sending it immediately. Still call
   `turn('chief', 'Chief', <text>)` immediately so the transcript is live and complete
   even before the mouth catches up — the transcript is not gated on speech pacing, only
   the actual voice injection is.

3. A small queue-pump function drains the queue ONE ITEM AT A TIME, only when the mouth
   isn't currently mid-response. Track "mouth busy" with a module-level (top-level
   `let`, alongside the existing `pc`/`stream`/`dc`/`live` declarations) boolean flag:
   set it `true` right after every `dc.send(JSON.stringify({ type: 'response.create'
   }))` you issue for this feature (both the first-sentence one and each queued one),
   and set it `false` in `handle()` when a `response.done` event arrives (add a new `if
   (ev.type === 'response.done')` branch — there isn't one today), then immediately try
   to drain the next queued item. When draining an item, send: `conversation.item.create`
   with `{type: 'message', role: 'user', content: [{type: 'input_text', text: '(Chief
   continues:) ' + <sentence text>}]}`, then `response.create` (set the busy flag before
   or as part of this send).

4. `done` event: record `out.full || out.spoken` into `lastAnswer` (same variable /
   same purpose as the old code's `lastAnswer = out.full || out.spoken`). No mouth
   message needs to be sent for `done` itself — the sentences already carried
   everything spoken. Set `setState('live', 'listening')` here (moving it from
   immediately after the old single fetch, to here, since now there's an in-flight
   stream + queue instead of one blocking call). If `done.failed` is true AND no
   `sentence` event was ever received for this turn (i.e., the very first thing that
   arrived was `done` with nothing before it — this happens on a hard failure before
   any text streamed), still speak the fallback exactly as before: send the
   `function_call_output` with `{answer: done.spoken}` and a `response.create`, since in
   that edge case nothing was said aloud yet. (If at least one `sentence` event already
   fired, the failure text was already streamed as a `sentence` event per the backend
   contract in Task 1, so don't double-send.)

5. If the network request itself fails (fetch throws, or a non-OK status, or the stream
   ends with no `done` event ever received): remove the holding bubble if still present,
   clear the queue, and send the exact same fallback the current failure path implies:
   `function_call_output` with `{answer: "Something went wrong on my end. Nothing's
   started."}` followed by `response.create`. This text must appear byte-for-byte
   identical to the backend's own failure string (already used in Task 1) — do not
   paraphrase it, this is what Neill hears, and it must never contain any technical
   detail about WHY it failed (no status codes, no error messages — those are for
   nobody, this file has no console/log surface Neill would see, but keep the
   convention anyway since this is the one file that talks to him).

6. Barge-in: add a new `if (ev.type === 'input_audio_buffer.speech_started')` branch in
   `handle()` that empties the queue array (`queue.length = 0`, or reassign to `[]` —
   either is fine) if Neill starts talking again while sentences are still queued. Do
   NOT try to cancel a response that's already mid-speech (no API call for that is in
   scope here) — just stop QUEUING further follow-ups so the mouth won't keep talking
   over him once the current utterance finishes.

7. Keep everything else in the file exactly as it is — the mic button, `start()`,
   `stop()`, the transcription-completed handler, the `response.output_audio_
   transcript.done` handler (still fires per mouth utterance, still calls `turn('chief',
   ...)` — note this means a given sentence's TEXT now typically appears twice in the
   transcript panel: once immediately from the `sentence` event per point 1/2, and once
   again when the mouth's own transcript of what it said comes back. That's an accepted
   minor duplication for this task — do not try to dedupe it, do not remove either call,
   it is out of scope and dedup risks losing the "even before the mouth catches up"
   liveness this task wants).
   Existing `error` handler stays as-is too.

Write clear, short comments in this file's existing plain style (it currently has none,
which is fine, but if you add non-obvious state like the busy flag / queue, one short
